WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.510
Film Courage: How to properly test your script?

00:00:01.510 --> 00:00:03.419
The absolute importance of script testing?

00:00:03.419 --> 00:00:05.350
What is script testing?

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:09.850
Corey Mandell, Screenwriter/Instructor: Script
testing…so when I was starting out a manager

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:13.549
did this for me and I’ll forever be grateful.

00:00:13.549 --> 00:00:17.539
So the way it works is most who write a script
they will give it to their friends, their

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:19.820
writing group, people they trust and they
want feedback.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:25.010
What’s working, what’s not working, suggestions,
ways to improve it.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:31.500
And what I was taught and I see a lot of the
more successful writers working this way is

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.530
that they don’t start there.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:41.050
What they start with is script testing and
what script testing is the first thing you

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:48.160
realize that when you write a script you experience
a movie, you experience characters.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.190
And when someone reads your script it replays
in their head.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.940
They experience the story and they experience
characters.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.600
And a lot of people make the assumption that
the movie that plays in my head is really

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.420
similar to the movie that plays in your head.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:07.210
Script testing is strategically asking a series
of questions to find out what movie is playing

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:08.870
in someone else’s head.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:10.320
What story are they watching?

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.390
What’s happening, why is it happening?

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.780
Why is it interesting or not interesting?

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.580
Who are the characters?

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.190
How are they experiencing the characters?

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:21.370
How would they describe the characters?

00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:22.880
Are they engaged with the characters?

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.380
Are they not engaged with the characters?

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.370
And it’s literally being able to track what
they are experiencing story and character

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:33.350
wise as they move through your script.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:39.780
What script testing aims for is not to know
if someone likes your script, loves it, hates

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.640
it, suggestions, critiques…not at first.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.340
First is are they experiencing what I’m
experiencing?

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:49.930
And if they’re not, why not?

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:53.210
What are they experiencing different from
me?

00:01:53.210 --> 00:02:00.110
When I first started teaching at UCLA I had
everyone bring in 7 copies of their script.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:05.250
Let’s say you and I would switch scripts
so I would give you 7 copies of my script,

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.230
you would give me 7 copies of your script.

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:11.230
You would take my 7 copies and go to 7 of
your friends and say “Hey would you read

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.020
a script by someone in my class.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:16.430
I haven’t read it and you don’t have to
have anything smart to say.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:17.580
You don’t have to give feedback.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:22.780
You just have to answer questions about what
you experienced when you read the script.”

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.010
And you really make it clear that you’re
not testing their reading comprehension, it’s

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:30.720
testing the writer’s ability to have it
come off the page.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.340
So let’s say 7 of your friends “Okay,
I’ll do that.”

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:40.400
So they read my script and then I would give
you a series of questions that you would ask

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.250
those writers and you would record the conversation.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:49.041
And it’s really important that you haven’t
read the script because you’ll ask the question

00:02:49.041 --> 00:02:52.990
about the story or the character and one of
your friends would start to give an answer

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:55.030
and then say “Is that right?”

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:56.030
And you’d say “I don’t know.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.010
I didn’t read the script.”

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:02.739
And so at some point they will just relax
and they will tell you what they experienced

00:03:02.739 --> 00:03:05.040
and you record that (all 7 of your friends).

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.840
Then you’d come back to class and give me
the recordings.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.180
I would do the same for you.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:15.349
I would have each writer transcribe the recordings
because you really have to hear it if you

00:03:15.349 --> 00:03:16.920
transcribe it.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.390
I have to admit when I first started teaching
at UCLA there were things that I did that

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.520
were successful and there were things…there
was a lot of trial and error when I first

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:25.760
started teaching.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:31.810
This was a real mixed bag because people were
so demoralized that they had a script they

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.880
poured heart and soul into, characters they
loved more than anything and this was there

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.620
character but everyone else was seeing this.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.840
And “this” was like “this” was despicable
and this was an amazing character.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.379
And a lot of people would come up to me and
they were like “Be careful with Julie in

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:51.980
the class because I think there is something
wrong with Julie because Julie’s friends

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.730
are like all schizophrenic…I’m telling
you because look at the feedback.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:56.940
Her friends are…”

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:58.300
So there was that.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:02.250
There was one person who just dropped out
of the MFA program, she did come back.

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.129
And then I had another one who threatened
to hurt herself.

00:04:05.129 --> 00:04:06.129
She didn’t!

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:11.220
But that is when I stopped doing this exercise
mandatory and I would make it voluntary.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:14.950
She did not hurt herself.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:19.559
A lot of people who came out of that class
went on to be working writers and said that

00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:26.159
this experience was the most painful experience
they had as a writer but the most valuable

00:04:26.159 --> 00:04:32.180
because they didn’t realize that not everything
that they were putting on the page was fully

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:39.219
coming off the page to other people and there
are so many people who write scripts that

00:04:39.219 --> 00:04:44.479
get rejected in the marketplace or they don’t
get an agent to represent them or a manager

00:04:44.479 --> 00:04:49.419
and they feel like “Okay my character or
story, something is not strong enough.”

00:04:49.419 --> 00:04:52.759
And that might be true and it might not be
true.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:59.229
It might be that if people actually fully
experienced your character and your story

00:04:59.229 --> 00:05:03.240
they would have fallen in love with it but
they didn’t.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.240
Here is a true story.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:10.870
Now this is not an average representation,
this is a little clean and easy.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:15.599
It’s not usually this clean and easy but
it will illustrate a point.

00:05:15.599 --> 00:05:23.599
So an agent sent me a writer not quite a year
ago (close to a year ago).

00:05:23.599 --> 00:05:27.550
And it was a really talented writer who was
staffed on some really prestigious shows but

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:30.180
had never been able to sell original content.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:35.099
Her stuff was great but it just wasn’t great
enough.

00:05:35.099 --> 00:05:43.919
So the manager had shown the script to some
people in the industry who trusted to unofficially

00:05:43.919 --> 00:05:45.379
get feedback on it.

00:05:45.379 --> 00:05:46.910
There were two main characters.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:51.189
They loved the concept, they loved the engine.

00:05:51.189 --> 00:05:54.059
But the second main character they found annoying.

00:05:54.059 --> 00:06:00.270
Like honestly if this character wasn’t in
the story, I would really be interested in

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:04.779
this but this character just ruined it for
me and that was hard for the writer to hear

00:06:04.779 --> 00:06:11.409
because this character was the main character
as far as she was concerned and it was sort

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:14.150
of an autobiographically, emotionally-based
character to her.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:17.389
So that always hurts because then they are
not only rejecting your writing, it feels

00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:20.050
like they are rejecting you.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:24.159
What I do when I work with a writer is instead
of reading their script and giving them my

00:06:24.159 --> 00:06:29.300
opinions I always start with I want to know
the movie that plays in their head.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:34.729
I want to know how they experience the story
and the characters to see where it’s not

00:06:34.729 --> 00:06:37.479
coming off of the page the way they see it.

00:06:37.479 --> 00:06:40.849
And I knew (because she shared with me) that
this character was the main issue).

00:06:40.849 --> 00:06:45.099
So I started with this character and just
asked her a series of questions, describe

00:06:45.099 --> 00:06:50.599
the character to me and then whatever she
said I said “Well why is the character this

00:06:50.599 --> 00:06:51.749
way?

00:06:51.749 --> 00:06:55.650
What does the character love more than anything
in the world and why is that?

00:06:55.650 --> 00:07:00.419
What do they fear more than anything in the
world and why is that?

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:03.279
When this happened in the script, why did
the character do it?

00:07:03.279 --> 00:07:05.399
And why do you find that so compelling?

00:07:05.399 --> 00:07:07.979
And when the character did this…why?”

00:07:07.979 --> 00:07:14.590
And so it was a good 30-40 minutes and when
we were done I could identify to pieces of

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:15.590
essential context.

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:19.080
Let me just stop and define how I would define
essential context.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:26.219
I would define essential context as everything
the audience (reader or viewer) everything

00:07:26.219 --> 00:07:32.719
the audience needs to know or experience to
understand what’s happening, why it’s

00:07:32.719 --> 00:07:34.919
happening and why they should care?

00:07:34.919 --> 00:07:38.399
And that’s true for the story and for the
character.

00:07:38.399 --> 00:07:42.539
I was able to identify two pieces of essential
context that were not in the script.

00:07:42.539 --> 00:07:46.360
There was something about the character’s
motivation for the main thing they were trying

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:50.039
to achieve that it wasn’t clear to a reader.

00:07:50.039 --> 00:07:55.509
And there was also something about this character’s
backstory I think that was really important.

00:07:55.509 --> 00:08:00.279
The writer kept talking about this as part
of why she cared so much about the character

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:02.060
and it wasn’t clear in the script.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.110
And at first the writer is like “Well of
course it’s clear.”

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:09.639
And I would ask them to highlight where it’s
clear and in doing that the writer was able

00:08:09.639 --> 00:08:13.289
to realize “Oh it’s implied.”

00:08:13.289 --> 00:08:17.209
But I could see now how some of them may not
get that.”

00:08:17.209 --> 00:08:19.990
And then the other piece of essential context
was gone.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:24.979
I’m sorry…in an original draft it was
there as she rewrote it and rewrote it somehow

00:08:24.979 --> 00:08:25.979
got removed.

00:08:25.979 --> 00:08:27.289
This was a really common experience.

00:08:27.289 --> 00:08:28.880
It just wasn’t there.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:33.570
And she didn’t catch that because when she
reads her script (the movie and characters

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.960
that play in her head) she knows all of this.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:37.130
She loves all of this.

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:41.450
She knows exactly…this character is her
so of course she knows exactly why the character

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.030
is doing it.

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:47.890
Now again this punchline is really clean and
simple.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:50.400
It’s not usually as clean and simple.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:57.060
But with this particular writer and again
three or four very senior people in the industry

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:01.440
at production companies had read the script
and said “Really like this, really love

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:06.240
this but this character ruins it for me.”

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.950
She just went in and she changed one line
of dialogue to make something crystal clear

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:13.360
and then she added a scene.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:18.400
It was about a two-page scene to get that
essential context across in the script.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:22.900
One line of dialogue and added a new scene
that was about two pages.

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:29.110
The purpose of these two pages was to make
this critical missing essential context clear.

00:09:29.110 --> 00:09:32.240
Film Courage: For the backstory?

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.710
Corey: Yes…actually it was her motivation.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:41.590
It was her line of dialogue for her backstory
but yes the two things that she knew that

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:47.770
we didn’t know or experience were remedied
with a change of dialogue and adding a two

00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:49.130
page scene.

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:54.360
Sent it to her manager…actually sent it
to a few of the people had already seen it

00:09:54.360 --> 00:10:00.390
and sent it to some new people and everybody
loved this character and said this is one

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:06.470
of my all time favorite characters and there
were actually multiple bids for that.

00:10:06.470 --> 00:10:13.000
Now unfortunately not usually as such after
you do script testing you realize I changed

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:17.140
one line of dialogue and I just added this
one little scene and the script goes from

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:22.780
a pass to multiple bids but that can be the
case.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:27.510
Usually there is more missing essential context
so it’s a little messier.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:32.460
So it’s usually multiple things that are
going to have to be addressed.

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:39.310
And that’s the whole key to script testing
is to figure out where people are not figuring

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:41.890
out what you’re intending and why.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:45.380
It almost always comes to essential context.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.900
And then it’s figuring out how to rewrite
the script.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:53.820
So hear is a true story that the writers have
publicly talked about.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:11:01.700
The writers of GAME OF THRONES…so a lot
of people probably know this (maybe some people

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:06.090
don’t) they originally shot that pilot and
then decided to redo the whole thing because

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:11.610
they didn’t like some of the casting and
so they literally reshot the pilot which gave

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:16.450
the show runners the opportunity to screen
the original pilot for people and they screened

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:21.090
it for some friends and they realized there
was a missing piece of essential context.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:27.360
So people who watched the original episode
(the original pilot that never aired) when

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:32.680
they got to the end, they knew that Cersei
(the queen) was having an affair with Jaime

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:42.730
who was her main bodyguard…there was a term
for what he was…the head of the queens guard

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:44.760
is his official term.

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:49.790
And that they were having inappropriate relations
because the queen is married to the king.

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:53.660
People didn’t know that that was her brother.

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:59.690
And that whole season doesn’t necessarily
work in the same way without knowing that

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:01.950
because that’s the stakes of the show.

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:07.430
The stakes of the show is that people go to
any length to make sure that nobody knows

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:13.430
that her children are illegitimate in that
way (illegitimate heirs to the throne).

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:18.150
So they caught that and then when of course
they went to redo the pilot they could rewrite

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:23.050
and reshoot that.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:27.780
This is what I was told, “only an idiot”
(this isn’t me talking, this was a manager

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:32.950
talking to me) “only an idiot would take
a script out to the marketplace that they

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:37.870
haven’t properly tested to see if you have
to rewrite, redesign, reshoot.”

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:45.580
And testing again is making sure that strangers
that don’t know what you’re going for

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:48.900
when they read your script do they experience
the story you experience?

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:51.660
Do they experience the characters you experience?

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:55.900
If so then you certainly want to know what
did you think?

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:57.100
Do you have suggestions?

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:58.100
Do you have critiques?

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:00.420
Where did it hold your interest?

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:06.270
You want to know all of that stuff after you
know that people are experiencing what you’re

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:07.270
experiencing.

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:11.380
In the workshop that I teach that I just came
from last night, we did an exercise where

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:15.150
they literally wrote a 3-4 page story.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:22.380
A scene but not a scene in a script, like
a short film (3-4 pages) as a exercise.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:26.230
And they have a main character who is trying
to achieve something and they have reasons

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:30.610
very motivated why (stakes to achieve it)
and there is another character who opposes

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:35.420
and they have really strong reasons why they
oppose (3-4 pages) and then everyone tested

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:42.620
their 3-4 pages to see if everyone could fully
see and experience all of the essential context

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:48.440
and with two exceptions everyone discovered
that there was at least one piece of essential

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:51.270
context that wasn’t being clear in 3-4 pages.

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:54.280
The two people who nailed it are both working
writers.

00:13:54.280 --> 00:13:58.390
I mean one is an executive producer and one
is a staff writer on a really successful show.

00:13:58.390 --> 00:14:00.100
So they’ve been trained that way.

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:04.040
So it is so important to test your scripts.

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:07.650
And if anyone wants the questions very easy.

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:12.640
Go to my website Corey Mandell (dot) net and
just sign up for the newsletter and the autoresponder

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:15.540
will give you the questions.

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:22.240
I highly recommend after you’ve poured heart
and soul into your script and when you read

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:28.500
it you’re like this is the movie or this
is the TV show I want to create.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.430
This is exactly what I am going for.

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:31.980
I love it!

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:38.280
Now go test it and see what other people are
experiencing because when it does go out to

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:40.560
the marketplace I want it to be your characters.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:41.650
I want it to be your story.

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:44.620
I want it to be the best possible version
of what you’re going for.

00:14:44.620 --> 00:14:46.180
So make sure by testing.

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:52.680
Film Courage: With the student that you had
where she wrote this sort of semi-autobiographical

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:57.900
story and it seemed like the sidekick character
was the likable one and the protagonist was

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:01.310
the one who people felt was off-putting.

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:07.380
Once she changed a few things - one line of
dialogue and a two-page scene you said?

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:11.840
Then people felt more empathy toward that
main character?

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:22.300
Corey: You know it would be like…so in real
life you meet someone and let’s say they

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:27.660
just rub you the wrong way and for whatever
reason you just don’t want to spend time

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:30.410
with them and you just don’t care about
them.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:36.020
But then you find out that they are really
struggling because they just lost their father

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:40.980
to cancer (a really terrible cancer) and they
are having troubles and their in that mourning

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:41.980
period.

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:46.779
And let’s just say that you lost your father
to cancer, you immediately feel different

00:15:46.779 --> 00:15:50.860
about that person and it changes your entire
dynamic.

00:15:50.860 --> 00:15:53.650
Now imagine that you meet this person and
you start to spend time with them and you

00:15:53.650 --> 00:15:57.779
never know that they just lost their father
to cancer, you are going to have a very different

00:15:57.779 --> 00:15:58.779
experience with them.

00:15:58.779 --> 00:16:08.200
So in this case her character when you really
understood why she was struggling, why she

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:13.390
was making some poor decisions it changed
how you felt about her it changed how you

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:15.060
understood it.

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:19.730
And people went from thinking this is a very
self-centered character who just treats people

00:16:19.730 --> 00:16:25.440
poorly, this is a character who is in a really
bad space and they’re doing their best and

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:29.290
actually they are trying to protect their
friend but they are doing it in the wrong

00:16:29.290 --> 00:16:33.550
way and it just changed how everyone felt
about that.

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:35.480
I mean it could be that subtle.

